+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — `fst` v0.4.7
+
+Property-based tests were added with [hegel] (`cargo add --dev hegeltest`,
+package `hegeltest` v0.28.2, library crate name `hegel`). Tests live in the
+existing test files: `tests/test.rs` (public `Set`/`Map` API),
+`src/raw/tests.rs` (raw `Builder`/`Fst` API), and `src/bytes.rs` (integer
+packing). No separate hegel-only test files were created.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+All properties are evidence-based; the evidence source is given for each.
+
+1. **`Set` agrees with a `BTreeSet` oracle** —
+   `tests/test.rs::hegel_props::prop_set_agrees_with_btreeset`.
+   Build a `Set` from sorted unique keys; `len`/`is_empty`, full stream
+   iteration (order and content), `contains` for every member, and `contains`
+   for a membership-biased probe key must all agree with `BTreeSet`.
+   Evidence: `Set` docs ("lexicographically ordered set of byte strings"),
+   `Set::from_iter` contract, existing `fst_set_*` unit tests. Key sets are
+   drawn with an explicit size up to 200+ (deep multi-level automata), mixing
+   a 3-symbol alphabet (deep shared prefixes) with arbitrary binary
+   (full byte range, empty key, invalid UTF-8).
+
+2. **`Map` agrees with a `BTreeMap` oracle** —
+   `tests/test.rs::hegel_props::prop_map_agrees_with_btreemap`.
+   Same shape for `Map` with arbitrary full-range `u64` values: `len`,
+   iteration of `(key, value)` pairs, `get`, `contains_key`, plus a biased
+   probe. Evidence: `Map` docs, existing `fst_map_*` unit tests.
+
+3. **Range queries agree with a `BTreeMap` oracle** —
+   `tests/test.rs::hegel_props::prop_map_range_agrees_with_btreemap`.
+   Any combination of `ge`/`gt`/`le`/`lt`/unbounded (bound keys biased toward
+   actual member keys, so inclusive/exclusive boundaries are hit constantly)
+   returns exactly the model's in-range pairs in order. Evidence:
+   `StreamBuilder` docs, the `test_range!` unit-test family in
+   `src/raw/tests.rs`.
+
+4. **Streaming set operations agree with a `BTreeSet` oracle** (4 tests, one
+   per operation, over 1–4 sets each) —
+   `tests/test.rs::hegel_props::prop_set_{union,intersection,difference,symmetric_difference}_agrees_with_oracle`.
+   Oracles: union of all sets; keys present in all sets; keys in the first
+   set present in no other (per `OpBuilder::difference` docs: "elements in the
+   first stream that don't exist in any other stream"); keys occurring in an
+   odd number of sets (per `OpBuilder::symmetric_difference` docs).
+   Evidence: `fst::set::OpBuilder` documentation and doc examples.
+
+5. **`Str(q).starts_with()` search = byte-prefix filter** —
+   `tests/test.rs::hegel_props::prop_prefix_search_agrees_with_filter`.
+   Searching a set with the prefix automaton returns exactly the keys with
+   `q` as byte prefix, in order. Queries are biased toward prefixes of actual
+   member keys. Evidence: `Str`/`StartsWith` docs and doc example.
+
+6. **`Subsequence(q)` search = naive subsequence filter** —
+   `tests/test.rs::hegel_props::prop_subsequence_search_agrees_with_filter`.
+   Oracle is a two-pointer subsequence check; queries biased toward true
+   subsequences of member keys. Evidence: `Subsequence` docs ("matches if the
+   input contains a specific subsequence").
+
+7. **Levenshtein automaton search = naive DP edit distance filter**
+   (`--features levenshtein`) —
+   `tests/test.rs::hegel_props::prop_levenshtein_search_agrees_with_naive`.
+   For a set of up to 50 keys over the alphabet `{a, b, c, é, ☃}` (the
+   multi-byte characters exercise the UTF-8 range machinery), query length
+   ≤ 6, distance ≤ 2: search results equal the keys whose character-level
+   Levenshtein distance to the query is ≤ d, computed by an independent
+   dynamic-programming oracle. Evidence: `Levenshtein` docs ("returns all
+   keys within a certain edit distance", Unicode-character based).
+
+8. **Raw FST write→read byte roundtrip** —
+   `src/raw/tests.rs::hegel_props::prop_fst_bytes_roundtrip`.
+   `Builder::insert` each model pair, `into_inner()` to raw bytes,
+   `Fst::new(bytes)` back: `verify()` (checksum) succeeds, `len` matches,
+   streaming returns exactly the model pairs, `get` returns each value.
+   Evidence: `Builder` docs ("representation of an fst is streamed to any
+   io::Write"), `Fst::new`/`verify` docs, `one_vec_multiple_fsts` unit test.
+
+9. **FST construction is deterministic** —
+   `src/raw/tests.rs::hegel_props::prop_fst_build_deterministic`.
+   Two `Builder`s fed the same sorted unique (key, value) pairs produce
+   byte-identical FSTs. Evidence: construction is a pure function of the
+   input sequence (the node registry hashes node contents with no randomized
+   seeds), and the CRC32C checksum footer checked by `verify()` presupposes
+   a deterministic serialization.
+
+10. **Builder ordering contract** —
+   `src/raw/tests.rs::hegel_props::prop_builder_insert_order_contract`.
+   After inserting a sorted-unique model, inserting a probe key that is less
+   than the last key fails with `Error::OutOfOrder`, equal fails with
+   `Error::DuplicateKey`, greater succeeds. Evidence: `Builder` docs ("All
+   keys must be added in lexicographic order... adding a duplicate key with an
+   output value will also result in an error") and the `test_map_fail!` /
+   `test_set_fail!` unit tests.
+
+11. **Port of the existing quickcheck property `prop_pack_in_out`** —
+    `src/bytes.rs::tests::prop_pack_in_out_hegel`.
+    `unpack_uint(pack_uint(n)) == n`. The quickcheck original capped values at
+    257; the hegel version covers the full `u64` range and explicitly boosts
+    every `pack_size` byte boundary (2^8k − 1, 2^8k, 2^8k + 1, 0, `u64::MAX`)
+    via `one_of!`. The quickcheck original is kept alongside (supplement, not
+    replace), so the `quickcheck`/`rand` dev-dependencies are untouched.
+
+Existing-PBT mapping (per the porting checklist): the crate has two
+quickcheck properties. `bytes::tests::prop_pack_in_out` is ported as (11).
+`raw::node::tests::prop_emits_inputs` (build set via `add`, stream emits
+sorted deduped inputs) is subsumed by (1), which exercises the same
+`Builder::add` path through `Set::from_iter` with broader inputs and stronger
+assertions; the quickcheck original is left in place.
+
+Not written: a stateful model test (the skill's Tier-1 pattern). An FST is
+immutable once built — the only "operations" are append-only sorted inserts
+followed by read-only queries, so the build-then-compare-to-oracle tests
+above are the natural specialization of a model test; there is no operation
+interleaving for a state machine to explore.
+
+## Test results
+
+- `cargo test` (default features): all pass — 101 lib tests (incl. 4 new
+  hegel tests), 12 integration tests (incl. 9 new hegel tests), doc tests.
+- `cargo test --features levenshtein`: all pass, including
+  `prop_levenshtein_search_agrees_with_naive`.
+- **Liveness checks**: `prop_set_agrees_with_btreeset` was temporarily broken
+  (`assert_eq!` → `assert_ne!` on the membership probe); hegel failed it and
+  shrank to the minimal counterexample `keys = {}`, probe `[]`. Likewise
+  `prop_fst_build_deterministic` (`assert_eq!` → `assert_ne!` on the two
+  byte buffers) failed and shrank to `model = {}`. Both breaks were reverted
+  and the `.hegel/` failure database deleted.
+- **Exploratory 10x run**: all hegel tests were temporarily raised to
+  `test_cases = 1000` and run under `--features levenshtein`; everything
+  still passed. For `prop_fst_build_deterministic` the raised run was
+  verified with a case counter (1030 case executions observed, ≥ 1000).
+  Committed tests use the default 100 cases.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+None found. Every property held at the default case count and at 10x. This
+checkout is upstream `fst` master (v0.4.7), a mature and widely-deployed
+crate whose core paths (build, get, range, streaming ops, Levenshtein) are
+already exercised by 100k-word unit tests, so a clean result is plausible
+rather than suspicious.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **`get_key(value)` as an inverse of `get`** was considered and rejected
+  before writing: the docs state behavior is *unspecified* unless values are
+  monotonically increasing in key order, so asserting it over arbitrary
+  values would test something the code never promised. A monotonic-values
+  version would be sound but was left out in favor of the properties above.
+- **Intersection/difference over zero streams** was avoided (generators draw
+  1–4 sets): the docs define `difference` "with respect to the first stream"
+  and the mathematical intersection of zero sets is not representable, so
+  behavior on an empty `OpBuilder` is not documented. Union over zero streams
+  would be sound (empty) but was not needed.
+- No written property had to be weakened or removed after running — no test
+  failure occurred other than the deliberate liveness break.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Overall the skill and the Rust reference worked as documented; every API used
+(`#[hegel::test]`, `#[hegel::composite]`, `one_of!`, `vecs`, `binary`,
+`sampled_from`, `booleans`, `integers`, `.boxed()`, `min_size`/`max_size`,
+`tc.assume`) compiled and behaved as described on the first attempt.
+Specific notes:
+
+- The "draw the size separately, then `min_size(n)` with no max" pattern for
+  large collections worked exactly as documented and was essential here
+  (multi-level automata need 100+ keys).
+- The `one_of!` note "box the branches with `.boxed()` only if the compiler
+  demands unification" was needed in practice: branches of different concrete
+  generator types (`vecs(...)` vs `binary()`) do demand `.boxed()`. Worth
+  stating that mixed-type branches *always* need it, since that is the common
+  case.
+- Gotcha 13 (no global case-count override) is accurate; the 10x exploratory
+  run required a temporary sed over the attributes. A per-run env override
+  would make that workflow much less invasive.
+- The liveness-check advice (break one property, watch it shrink, then
+  delete `.hegel/`) worked exactly as written; the shrunk counterexample was
+  genuinely minimal (`{}` / `[]`).
+- The helper-function-taking-`&TestCase` pattern from the reference
+  (`draw_probe(&tc, &model)`) was a good fit for member-biased probe/bound
+  generation and kept composites to the three reusable generators.
+- One small gap: the skill's Tier-1 "model test" guidance assumes a mutable
+  data structure. A sentence acknowledging immutable/build-once structures
+  (where the model test degenerates to build-vs-oracle comparison, as here)
+  would save deliberation.
